    <string name="calc_band">%1$s band</string>
    <string name="calc_outside_bands">Outside the amateur bands</string>
    <string name="calc_k_factor">Shortening factor K</string>
    <string name="calc_k_note">K accounts for end effect and wire insulation: 0.95 for bare wire (formula 142.5/f), about 0.92–0.93 for insulated wire.</string>
    <string name="calc_dipole_title">Half-wave dipole</string>
    <string name="calc_total_length">Total length</string>
    <string name="calc_leg">A – leg (×2)</string>
    <string name="calc_wavelength">Wavelength λ</string>
    <string name="calc_dipole_tip1" formatted="false">Cut each leg ~3–5 % longer and trim until the SWR minimum falls on the chosen frequency.</string>
    <string name="calc_dipole_tip2">Inverted V (90–120° angle): start with the same dimensions – it usually needs shortening by a few percent.</string>
    <string name="calc_dipole_tip3">Impedance about 50–75 Ω; a 1:1 current balun at the feed point limits currents on the braid.</string>
    <string name="calc_efhw_title">EFHW – end-fed half-wave</string>
    <string name="calc_radiator">A – radiator</string>
    <string name="calc_counterpoise">B – counterpoise</string>
    <string name="calc_transformer">Transformer</string>
    <string name="calc_efhw_harmonics">Also works on harmonics: %1$s MHz.</string>
    <string name="calc_efhw_tip1" formatted="false">Start with a wire ~3 % longer and trim on the lowest band; set the higher bands with the capacitor at the transformer.</string>
    <string name="calc_efhw_tip2">The coax braid often acts as the counterpoise – a choke about 0.05 λ from the transformer keeps RF out of the shack.</string>
    <string name="calc_gp_title">Quarter-wave ground plane</string>
    <string name="calc_radials">B – radials (3–4 pcs)</string>
    <string name="calc_gp_tip1">Radials sloping at about 45° give about 50 Ω; horizontal ones about 36 Ω (SWR ~1.4).</string>
    <string name="calc_gp_tip2">Radials lying on the ground need not be resonant – the more, the better.</string>
    <string name="calc_gp_tip3">Tune with the radiator length; leave the radials slightly longer.</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_title">J-pole antenna</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_long">A – long element (¾λ)</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_short">B – short element (¼λ)</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_feed">C – feed point from the bottom</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_spacing">D – element spacing</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_tip1">Move the feed point (C) up/down looking for the SWR minimum – this is the main adjustment.</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_tip2">Resonance is set by the length of element A; B has less influence.</string>
    <string name="calc_jpole_tip3">A choke (a few turns) on the cable below the antenna helps – a J-pole likes currents on the braid.</string>
    <string name="calc_yagi_title">3-element Yagi</string>
    <string name="calc_reflector">R – reflector</string>
    <string name="calc_driven">W – driven element</string>
    <string name="calc_director">D – director</string>
    <string name="calc_spacing_rw">S1 – R–W spacing</string>
    <string name="calc_spacing_wd">S2 – W–D spacing</string>
    <string name="calc_boom">Boom length</string>
    <string name="calc_yagi_tip1">Starting dimensions for tube elements; gain typically 7–8 dBi, front-to-back ratio 15–20 dB.</string>
    <string name="calc_yagi_tip2">The driven element has an impedance of about 20–30 Ω – matching is needed (gamma, hairpin, beta).</string>
    <string name="calc_yagi_tip3">For a specific tube diameter it is best to recalculate the design in software (e.g. YagiCAD, 4nec2).</string>
    <string name="calc_wire_diameter">Wire / tube diameter</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_title">Moxon rectangle</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_a">A – width</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_b">B – driven element bend</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_c">C – gap</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_d">D – reflector bend</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_e">E – depth (B+C+D)</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_tip1">L.B. Cebik\'s formulas (dependent on wire diameter) – fed directly with 50 Ω coax at the top (driven element).</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_tip2">Gain about 5.5–6 dBi, very good front-to-back ratio; keep gap C exact.</string>
    <string name="calc_moxon_tip3" formatted="false">Insulated wire behaves like a thicker, “slower” one – shorten the dimensions by 2–3 %.</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_title">Full-wave loop (Quad / Delta)</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_perimeter">Loop perimeter (driven)</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_quad">A – square side (Quad)</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_delta">B – triangle side (Delta)</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_reflector">Reflector perimeter (2-el Quad)</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_tip1">Formula 306.3/f (1005/f ft) for wire; impedance about 100–120 Ω.</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_tip2">Matching to 50 Ω: a λ/4 section of 75 Ω coax (“Cable sections” tab).</string>
    <string name="calc_loop_tip3">Fed at the bottom – horizontal polarisation; at a corner/side – vertical.</string>
